  "caveats": [
   "GSC totals (no dimension / date / device / country) are property-level aggregates; by-language and top-pages figures are page-level aggregates (summed from page rows), so they do not reconcile exactly with totals.",
   "GSC anonymises rare queries: query and query+page rows omit them, so query-level sums are well below page/property totals (query rows here sum to 104 clicks / 6605 impressions vs 482 / 23075 property totals).",
   "GSC dataState=all includes provisional data for the last 2-3 days (2026-10-15..17); these may still change.",
   "Language prefix \"/\" = root homepage plus legacy /article/*.html URLs.",
   "Position is impression-weighted average; for language aggregates it is computed from page rows.",
   "GA4 counts only consented sessions (Consent Mode / CMP); GA4 sessions understate real traffic and are not comparable 1:1 with GSC clicks. GA4 includes non-Google sources (Bing, ChatGPT, Yahoo...).",
   "GA4 landingPage \"(not set)\" = sessions without a page_view (often consent/late-tag artefacts).",
   "Small samples: most pages have <50 clicks in the window; treat CTR deltas as directional.",
   "Spike from 2026-10-13 coincides with election-related content and the autumn/mushroom season; baseline is not seasonally stable.",
   "No PII: aggregated data only.",
   "Tooling: GSC rows were pulled straight from the Search Console API (read-only, the gsc MCP server's saved OAuth token), because the MCP get_advanced_search_analytics tool ignores sort_by, and a page filter combined with a non-page dimension (device) returned undercounted totals (e.g. /es/ = 76 clicks vs 278 from page rows). Do not rebuild the by-language split with that MCP filter.",
   "page x device / page x country rows from the API are heavily thinned (they come back far below page totals), so no per-page device split is included.",
   "GA4 google/organic sessions (215) vs GSC clicks (482) imply roughly 45% of Google clicks become a consented GA4 session; treat GA4 as a sample."
  ],
